= Staal's Influence

Although Staal insists he has no intention of establishing a military dictatorship and claims the Commonwealth Army is merely stabilizing the nation in its time of need, we suspect he is maneuvering to consolidate power. This, of course, involves the infiltration of his supporters into the Greater Worker's Guild and the quiet persuasion of <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> leaders through shady deals and under-the-table arrangements. It is already evident that our decision-making is being compromised.

And the longer we watch him work, the harder it becomes to believe that "stabilizing the nation" is the whole of it. Staal is not making the Commonwealth strong to keep it standing on its own; he is making it strong and orderly enough to be worth something at a bargaining table—and there is only one table he can mean. He has never said the word, and he likely never will, but the drift of all his talk of strength and order points north: toward the Empress's court, toward the reunion the old republic was founded to refuse.

What unsettles us most is how little the prospect seems to alarm anyone else. A decade of crisis has worn the country to the bone, and to a great many Commonwealthers a return to the Empire that promised an end to the chaos no longer sounds like surrender—it sounds like rest. That is Staal's quiet genius: he frightens no one. He is not Gallax, raving of purges and crusades; he is the reasonable general who will work with anyone but the <span style="color: #700000;">Collectivists</span> and asks only for calm. The committed democrats among us see it plainly, and say it to any who will listen: he is gentler than Gallax, and the destination is the same. Whichever of the two the Commonwealth follows, our democracy does not survive the journey.

- @root: Ok.
